  "description": "health.v2 §2.5, §5: \"is this service's clock ahead?\". Computed twice by two scripts of the text's drafter that share no code, kept out of the repository, and never blessed. `heartbeats`: a put on the heartbeat key covering health.v2, its `payload` in hex (core §7.5's form 6: CH, the run, the seq, the body, read with the standard contract's one collection, state/status, of one member), the tag of the revision the reader holds (`holds_tag`; revision 2.0's is 1) and the stamp of the last heartbeat it read on that key (`last_read`, <run>/<seq>, or null for none) -> {read: \"instant\" | \"ahead\" | \"unknown\"}, the time of a read heartbeat (freshness.v2 §2.5), or {unread: \"revision\" | \"refused\" | \"older\"}, a heartbeat its steps 1 to 3 stop, which counts for nothing. The sections play no part: a cleared bit, a digest that is not the view's and a map bit of 0 change nothing. `cases`: what one reader heard of one service, in arrival order -> the answer. presence, descriptor and face are judgements.json's. heard: \"heartbeat\" (a read heartbeat covering health.v2, or a read owner heartbeat of the host class, whose time is an instant), \"heartbeat_ahead\" (the same, its time the ahead time), \"heartbeat_unknown\" (the same, its time unknown), \"heartbeat_unread\" (a heartbeat or an owner heartbeat not read), \"status_put\", \"status_deleted\", \"clock_ahead\" (a stream/faults sample whose code is 1, whatever its level and its argument) or \"fault\" (one with any other code). The last read heartbeat answers: heartbeat_ahead yes, `ahead`; heartbeat no, `confirmed`; heartbeat_unknown unobservable, `time_unknown`; none read unobservable, `nothing_heard`. Nothing else answers: a clock_ahead fault is an occurrence, published once, and says nothing of whether the hold lasts. expect.answer is yes, no, unobservable or not_asked (absent, `absent`; a descriptor that lists neither major, `not_listed`). expect.major is judgements.json's: under 1 the case expects nothing more, the question being health.v1's. `heartbeat` gives what the `heartbeats` payloads were built from: the key, the collection, its one member's key and stamp, and that member's digest (core §7.5).",
